<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 8th Grade (CEFR: A2.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Books and Movies</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> My Favorite Movie Is... Because...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow audio narratives. Receptive: Show recognition of specific details when listening to explanations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow audio narratives by responding to questions in discussions about movies and books, showing comprehension of specific preferences (e.g., "Why does Jose like this book?" "Because it has amazing characters").<br>Can identify key points shared by classmates when listening to explanations or participating in discussions (e.g., "Who likes action movies?" "Juan").<br>Can answer simple questions about favorite genres after listening to peers (e.g., "What type of book does Maria like?" "Mysteries").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing images of various movie posters and book covers (e.g., action, comedy, fantasy, mystery, documentary, poetry). Students briefly say "movie" or "book" for each, and then share one word they associate with it (e.g., "fun," "scary," "exciting"). This activates prior knowledge of genres and preferences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the target language for expressing preferences and reasons. Teacher points to a movie poster and says: "I like watching action movies because they are exciting." Then to a book cover: "I prefer reading mystery books over poetry because mysteries are more engaging." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of "watches" /ɪz/ and "runs" /z/ while describing preferences, and practices `full` /ʊ/ vs `fool` /uː` with familiar words. The teacher defines key vocabulary like `genre`, `character`, `plot`, `entertaining`, `engaging` through gestures and simple definitions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the target grammar and vocabulary.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I like watching action movies because they are exciting,' am I talking about something I enjoy or something I dislike?" (Expected answer: "Enjoy.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'genre' mean? Is it the main character or the type of movie/book?" (Expected answer: "The type of movie/book.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Books and Movies".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (approx. 1.5 minutes) between two teenagers, Jose and Maria, discussing their favorite movies and books and why. The dialogue naturally incorporates `Present simple to describe preferences`, `verb + -ing + noun + because`, `Past simple` (e.g., "I watched a comedy yesterday. It was about..."), `comparing preferences` (e.g., "I prefer action movies over comedies because they are more thrilling."). It uses vocabulary: `movie`, `book`, `genre`, `character`, `plot`, `theme`, `documentary`, `enjoy`, `watch`, `like`, `prefer`, `entertaining`, `engaging`, `emotional`, `favorite`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow audio narratives and show recognition of specific details when listening to explanations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listen (Gist):</b> Students listen for general understanding. "What are Jose and Maria talking about?" (Expected: "Their favorite movies and books.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listen (Specific Details - True/False):</b> Students listen again and mark statements as True (T) or False (F).
                            <ul>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Jose likes watching action movies. (T)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Maria enjoys reading fantasy books because of the good plot. (F - good characters)</li>
                                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Jose watched a documentary yesterday. (F - comedy)</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third Listen (Information Matching):</b> Students listen for specific preferences and reasons, matching names to genres and reasons from a provided list. (e.g., "Why does Jose like this book?" "Because it has amazing characters.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Present simple to describe preferences and reasons (`verb + -ing + noun + because`), Past simple for recent movie/book experiences, comparing preferences (`I prefer... over... because...`). Vocabulary: `movie`, `book`, `poetry`, `character`, `plot`, `theme`, `genre`, `express`, `describe`, `watch`, `enjoy`, `like`, `prefer`, `entertaining`, `engaging`, `emotional`, `favorite`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listen and Fill):</b> The teacher plays 5-6 short, distinct audio sentences or phrases. Each snippet is a preference statement from a different speaker. Students listen and fill in the missing word(s) in a provided sentence frame (e.g., "I like ____ing documentaries because they are ____." or "Yesterday, I ____ a comedy. It was about ____."). This activity focuses on identifying target vocabulary and grammar structures when listening.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Listen and Choose):</b> The teacher plays 5-6 sentences, each expressing a preference (e.g., "She prefers reading fantasy books."). After each sentence, the teacher provides two options (e.g., "Does she like action movies or fantasy books?"). Students listen and choose the correct option by raising a hand or pointing to an answer. This reinforces listening for specific details within a known structure. The teacher explicitly models how to identify `s`, `z`, and `ɪz` sounds in present tense verbs like `watches`, `reads`, `likes`.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Listening and Preference Check:</b> Students work in pairs (A and B). Student A has a list of three preferences (e.g., "Likes watching animated movies," "Prefers reading detective stories," "Enjoys emotional plots"). Student B has a blank grid. Student A briefly *says* their preferences (e.g., "I like watching animated movies because they are funny.") using the target structures. Student B *listens* and checks off the preferences listed by Student A on their grid. Then they switch roles. This is a listening comprehension check on peer output.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Books and Movies".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Listening for Preferences Scavenger Hunt." Students will listen to short audio clips of three different students (pre-recorded by teacher or advanced students) describing their favorite movie/book and giving a reason. Each audio clip uses the target grammar (Present simple, verb + -ing + noun + because, past simple if applicable for a recent watch, comparing preferences) and vocabulary (genre, character, plot, entertaining, emotional). The students' task is to listen carefully to each clip and complete a "Preference Profile" sheet for each speaker.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher plays three separate short audio clips (30-45 seconds each) of different students (e.g., "Speaker 1," "Speaker 2," "Speaker 3") describing their favorite movie or book, and why.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a "Preference Profile" worksheet with sections for each speaker: "Speaker's Favorite Genre," "Speaker's Reason (Key Details)," and "Did they watch/read something recently? What was it?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen to each clip (played twice if needed) and fill in the information on their worksheet. They must identify the key points shared by each speaker, answer simple questions about favorite genres, and note specific preferences.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After listening to all clips, students briefly compare their answers in small groups to confirm understanding, focusing on listening comprehension and identifying key details. This allows students to engage in `Participating in discussions` by comparing their listening notes and `Giving opinions` about what they heard.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Preference Profile" completion and collection of the completed worksheets. The teacher will observe students' ability to follow audio narratives and identify specific details. For `Sociolinguistic` competence, the teacher observes if students provide constructive feedback by clarifying or confirming information with peers during group comparison.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' completed "Preference Profile" worksheets from the Performance stage.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Listening):</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Identifies favorite genre for each speaker (2 points per speaker).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Records at least one specific reason/detail for each preference (2 points per speaker).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes if a recent watch/read is mentioned (1 point per speaker).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows overall comprehension of the audio narratives.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the easiest part about listening to the descriptions today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or phrase you learned or heard today?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 8px;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Connecting to future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now about understanding someone talking about their favorite movies or books?" (Scale of 1-5, or thumbs up/down).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do I need more practice with to improve my listening skills?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? (e.g., The audio quality, the clarity of the task, students' engagement with the content).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next listening activity or future lessons based on student performance and engagement today? (e.g., More pre-teaching of specific vocabulary, shorter audio clips, more varied listening task types).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), students will engage with a written text that uses the same vocabulary and grammatical structures to describe movies and books, allowing them to reinforce their understanding of the content introduced through listening today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>